// Paths whose endpoints call a recursive operation of a data block.
//
// The kernel replays each path that the checker admits. So the two engines
// must reduce a call of the operation at an endpoint to the same value.
//
// The operation's rules compute the call. A path by reflexivity reads the
// call at its endpoint, and the checker and the kernel reduce it to one value.
//
// - see: #4291 — `zeroPlusZero`, `zeroIsZeroPlusZero`, `onePlusOne`

/// The naturals in unary, with an addition that its rules compute.
data NatUnits : Type where {
  /// Zero.
  | Zero : NatUnits
  /// The successor of `n`.
  | Succ : (n : NatUnits) --> NatUnits
  /// The sum of `m` and `n`, which the two rules compute by recursion on `m`.
  oper add : (m : NatUnits, n : NatUnits) --> NatUnits;
  /// Zero plus `n` is `n`.
  rule : add Zero n <=> n;
  /// The successor of `m`, plus `n`, is the successor of `m` plus `n`.
  rule : add (Succ m) n <=> Succ (add m n);
}

/// The first rule makes zero plus zero zero.
def zeroPlusZero : Path(add Zero Zero, Zero) = Path.here Zero;

/// The call stands at the right endpoint. The point is its value.
def zeroIsZeroPlusZero : Path(Zero, add Zero Zero) = Path.here Zero;

/// One plus one is two. The second rule calls the operation again, and the
/// first rule ends the call.
def onePlusOne : Path(add (Succ Zero) (Succ Zero), Succ (Succ Zero)) = Path.here (Succ (Succ Zero));
